      <h1>Lithium Cold-Weather Performance — Capacity, Charging and Heating</h1>
      <div class="takeaways"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Cold reduces available capacity and charge acceptance, and charging a lithium cell below 0 °C can cause lithium plating. A cold-climate installation needs a heated pack, a lower charge rate, or a chemistry and BMS rated for the temperature.</div>

<h2>What cold does to a lithium cell</h2>
<p>At low temperature the electrolyte conducts less well and the kinetics slow down. The immediate effects are higher internal resistance, lower available capacity and a voltage that sags under load. The dangerous effect is charging: below 0 °C, lithium can plate onto the anode instead of intercalating, permanently reducing capacity and creating a safety risk.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Temperature</th><th>Typical effect</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>25 °C</td><td>Reference performance</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>0 °C</td><td>Capacity down ~10–20%; charge acceptance falls</td><td>Reduce charge rate; enable low-temp charge limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>−10 °C</td><td>Capacity down ~20–30%; charging usually blocked</td><td>Heat the pack before charging</td></tr>
<tr><td>−20 °C</td><td>Capacity down ~30–40%; discharge only</td><td>Insulate; consider a different chemistry</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Charging below zero</h2>
<p>Most LFP and NMC BMS units block charging below 0 °C, and that is a feature, not a fault. If a system charges anyway, the risk is plating. The correct solution is to warm the pack — with a heater mat, a warm enclosure or a charge profile that waits — not to override the limit.</p>
<h2>Heated packs</h2>
<p>A heated pack consumes energy to stay warm. In a solar or off-grid system, that energy must be included in the load calculation. Insulation and placing the pack inside the thermal envelope are usually cheaper than heating.</p>
<h2>Storage in cold climates</h2>
<p>Store at partial state of charge, above the minimum voltage, and avoid charging until the pack is above 0 °C. A pack stored at −20 °C is not damaged by the cold itself; it is damaged by charging while cold.</p>
<h2>Lead-acid for comparison</h2>
<p>Lead-acid loses cranking current in the cold — a battery rated 600 CCA at −18 °C may deliver far less at −30 °C — but it can be charged at low temperature, slowly. The trade-off is real: lithium is lighter and more efficient, lead-acid is more forgiving of cold charging.</p>

